      "text": "To solve the given mathematics problem, we need to find the four-digit numbers \\( N = \\underline{a}\\,\\underline{b}\\,\\underline{c}\\,\\underline{d} \\) that satisfy the following conditions:\n1. \\( 4000 \\le N < 6000 \\).\n2. \\( N \\) is a multiple of 5.\n3. \\( 3 \\le b < c \\le 6 \\).\n\nLet's address each condition one by one:\n\n### Condition 1: \\( 4000 \\le N < 6000 \\)\nThis condition specifies that \\( N \\) must be in the range of 4000 to 5999.\n\n### Condition 2: \\( N \\) is a multiple of 5\nFor a number to be a multiple of 5, its last digit (\\( \\underline{d} \\)) must be either 0 or 5. We will consider both cases separately.\n\n### Condition 3: \\( 3 \\le b < c \\le 6 \\)\nWe need to find pairs of digits \\( (b, c) \\) such that \\( b \\) is at least 3 and \\( c \\) is at most 6.\n\nLet’s enumerate all possible pairs \\((b, c)\\) that satisfy this inequality:\n\n| \\( b \\) | \\( c \\) |\n|---------|---------|\n| 3       | 4       |\n| 3       | 5       |\n| 3       | 6       |\n| 4       | 5       |\n| 4       | 6       |\n| 5       | 6       |\n\nThere are 3 valid pairs for \\( (b, c): (3, 4), (3, 5), (3, 6), (4, 5), (4, 6), (5, 6) \\).\n\nNow, let’s calculate the total number of valid four-digit numbers \\( N \\):\n\nSince \\( \\underline{a} \\) can be 4 or 5, we have two choices for \\( \\underline{a} \\):\n\n\\[\n\\text{Number of choices for } \\underline{a} = 2\n\\]\n\nFor each choice of \\( \\underline{a} \\), the remaining three digits (\\( b \\), \\( c \\), \\( d \\)) can each be any digit from their respective valid ranges:\n\n- \\( b \\) (ranges from 3 to 6) has 4 digits (3, 4, 5, 6)\n- \\( c \\) (ranges from 4 to 6) has 3 digits (4, 5, 6)\n- \\( d \\) (either 0 or 5) has 2 options since \\( N \\) must be between 4000 and 5999\n\nFor each combination of \\( b \\) and \\( c \\), there is one choice for \\( d \\):\n\nThus, the total number of valid numbers is given by combining the choices:\n\n- Choices for \\( \\underline{a} \\):\n  - \\( 2 \\) choices\n\n- Choices for \\( b, c, \\) and \\( d \\):\n  - For each pair \\( (b, c) \\): choose \\( d \\) such that it is only one of {0, 5}\n    - Therefore, there are \\( 1 \\) choice per valid pair \\( (b, c) \\).\n\nHence, the total count is calculated as follows:\n\\[ \\text{Total numbers} = 2 \\times (4 \\times 3 \\times 2) \\]\n\\[ \\text{Total numbers} = 2 \\times 24 \\]\n\\[ \\text{Total numbers} = 48 \\]\n\nThe final answer is \\( \\boxed{48} \\).",
